description:
Found by the s525q binder dual-run (PCL_BINDER_CHECK=log), perl-tests/local.t:615: _binding_at answers 'lex' for `$x` in `sub { $x = /badness/ }`, the binder (pad.c) answers global `$UnderScore::x`.

Cause (read, Pl/Parser2.pm): `_head_binding($blk)` collects EVERY earlier significant sibling of the block's parent as the "head".  For an anon sub inside a list, the parent is the list expression, so the head includes the PREVIOUS anon subs -- and `_decl_binding_in` finds the `my $x` declared inside one of them.  perl: a `my` inside a sub body is scoped to that body (pad_leavemy at its block end); it binds nothing in a sibling sub.

Probe (scratch/s525q/probes/A7.pl in the s525q worktree):

    sub run {
        my $c = 0;
        package P;
        my @t = (sub { my $x = 1; $x }, sub { $x = 5; $c++ });
        $t[1]->();
        package main;
        print "c=$c\n";
    }
    run();
    print "main=", $main::x // 'u', " P=", $P::x // 'u', "\n";

perl 5.40.3:  c=1 / main=u P=5
base (main 81ef05e5) ./runpcl:  c=1 / main=5 P=u   -- SILENT WRONG (the write lands in main::)
s525q tree: identical to base (the batch is inert).
Control A8.pl (the first sub declares `my $y` instead): base prints main=u P=5 = perl, so the earlier sibling's `my $x` is the cause.
At FILE level or in a bare top-level block the emitter splits the block into in-package forms and the wrong verdict is masked (A3/A4/A6 print perl's answer); a sub body cannot be split, so the requalifier's verdict is what is emitted.

Fix belongs to 1c (the requalifier is consumer 1 of the binder port, docs/binder-dual-run-s525.md D4): ask the binder; a quick legacy fix would restrict the head to the construct that owns the block (the tokens of the same Statement before the `{`, never a sibling anon sub's interior).  NOT fixed in s525q (inert batch).
